00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:15.270
The South Pole is, without a doubt, one of
the most extreme places on earth that’s

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:17.570
continuously inhabited by humans.

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:21.730
In fact, it’s safe to say that there’s
nowhere in earth with such a large population—about

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:28.659
45 in the winter and 150 in the summer—where
nature is so constantly working to kill you.

00:00:28.659 --> 00:00:33.649
The warmest it’s ever been at the pole,
in recorded history, is just 10 degrees Fahrenheit,

00:00:33.649 --> 00:00:38.309
or -12 degrees Celsius—a temperature that
most of the world would consider absolutely

00:00:38.309 --> 00:00:44.280
unbearable—while most of the year is spent
at 50 or 60 below zero, but it’s not just

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:47.520
temperature that gives the pole its “extreme”
classification.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:53.640
It’s also one of the driest places on earth,
with a relative humidity regularly dipping

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:54.879
to 0.03%.

00:00:54.879 --> 00:01:00.910
In comparison, most places’ humidity hovers
between 55% and 75%, meaning that those at

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:05.540
the South Pole are constantly battling skin
and body dehydration.

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:10.200
In addition to all that, due to the thousands
of feet of ice and snow layered on top of

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:16.170
the ground below, the Pole is located at about
9,300 feet or 2,800 meters above sea level,

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:19.570
which for most people isn’t high enough
to be deadly, but is enough to potentially

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:23.759
lead to sickness which could elevate the severity
of other conditions.

00:01:23.759 --> 00:01:29.729
Put together, these factors compound to paint
a pretty clear picture: the South Pole was

00:01:29.729 --> 00:01:36.399
not made for humans, but nonetheless, through
sheer force of will, we’re there, permanently.

00:01:36.399 --> 00:01:41.070
What makes this possible is the Amundsen-Scott
South Pole Station.

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:44.810
This American-run facility is now in its third
iteration.

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:49.920
The first was built in 1956 as a simple, wooden,
pre-fabricated structure that quickly became

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:51.340
buried by snow.

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:56.700
This was upgraded in 1974 to a larger geodesic
dome that itself protected buildings inside,

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:01.460
but this too was buried by snow each winter,
meaning that each summer, considerable time

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:04.130
and fuel had to be used to dig the station
out.

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:09.150
In a place where absolutely everything has
to be flown in, this was an enormous expense

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:11.660
that got in the way of the science.

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:16.300
Therefore, in the years leading up to the
turn of the millennia, the United States Antarctic

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:20.430
Program worked to develop a more permanent
solution.

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:22.790
And that solution was this.

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:27.770
Now, this current structure is undoubtably
distinctive, but its design comes with good

00:02:27.770 --> 00:02:28.780
reason.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:33.430
The building itself is shaped like an airplane
wing, with the leading edge facing the prevailing

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:34.430
winds.

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:38.910
This pushes air down, accelerating it underneath
the facility, which naturally clears out the

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:39.910
snow.

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:44.840
As most of Antarctica is a desert, only about
8 inches or 20 centimeters of snow per year

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:49.420
accumulates at the South Pole, but as the
temperature quite literally never goes above

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:52.080
freezing, the snow never melts.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:56.260
In addition, since the landscape surrounding
the Pole is quite flat, and the winds are

00:02:56.260 --> 00:03:00.990
strong, enormous snow banks quickly form on
the upwind side of any building.

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:05.330
While the shape of the main station building
reduces this, snow still does accumulate in

00:03:05.330 --> 00:03:06.920
front and under it.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:11.110
Therefore, it’s designed so that, every
ten to fifteen years, the structure will be

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:13.260
raised up a few feet.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:17.020
This is a thirty-day process, which can only
happen over the three-month summer period

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:20.690
when the station can be reached by plane,
meaning that in order to not shut down the

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:25.370
facility during its busiest period, the building
was designed to be operational while it’s

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:26.370
lifted.

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:30.150
Therefore, its different components are connected
by a number of flexible joints that can move

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:32.030
during the lifting process.

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:37.920
Of course, the actual purpose of the station
is for research, but that poses the question,

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:41.980
what sort of research do you actually need
to be at the South Pole for?

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:45.160
Well, as it turns out, quite a lot.

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:47.160
One of the top uses is for astronomy.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:52.520
You see, in most places, water vapor in the
air ever so slightly distorts the image from

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:56.420
telescopes, which for most types of astronomy
isn’t a problem, but when one is trying

00:03:56.420 --> 00:04:01.520
to observe galaxies billions of light years
away, for example, precision is key.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:08.570
In fact, the South Pole telescope itself has
discovered truly countless far-away galaxies.

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:13.040
In addition, thanks to this clarity, it was
a crucial component of the planet-wide network

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:18.609
of telescopes that created the first-ever
direct image of a black hole in 2019.

00:04:18.609 --> 00:04:23.050
Beyond that, the clean air of the pole allows
for super-precise atmospheric research, and

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:28.319
the relatively low annual accumulation allows
for climate research through studying ice-cores.

00:04:28.319 --> 00:04:32.590
This is all to say, the South Pole station
isn’t just there for prestige.

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:39.110
It’s a critical piece of scientific infrastructure
supporting a huge variety of research.

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:44.400
However, most of the research itself isn’t
actually conducted there.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:48.330
Given how expensive it is to travel to the
pole, various institutions generally only

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:52.650
send a small number of people to the station
to calibrate and maintain their scientific

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:57.150
equipment, while the data are sent back to
their respective headquarters’ for analysis.

00:04:57.150 --> 00:05:01.360
That means that, each summer, in that three-month
window when flights can land, there’s a

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:06.419
flurry of activity as personnel try to get
all their work done before the season is over.

00:05:06.419 --> 00:05:11.260
Then, over winter, only a skeleton crew remains
to keep everything running.

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:16.569
Of course, given the absolute isolation that
occurs over this period, the station staff

00:05:16.569 --> 00:05:19.009
is very carefully selected.

00:05:19.009 --> 00:05:22.650
Everyone is screened to assure that they’re
in near-perfect psychical health, as conditions

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:26.580
that might be minor in the rest of the world
can turn deadly given the station’s lack

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:31.300
of advanced medical care, and they’re also
subject to a extensive psychological evaluation.

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:35.780
This is primarily to assure that they can
handle the severe degree of isolation, six-months

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:41.300
of continuous darkness, and small work community
for nine months as, if mental health deteriorates

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:45.639
over winter, there’s truly no way out.

00:05:45.639 --> 00:05:49.879
Over this period, there tends to only be a
single doctor at the station, conducting their

00:05:49.879 --> 00:05:52.699
work with somewhat limited medical facilities.

00:05:52.699 --> 00:05:56.580
This doctor is subject to even more rigorous
medical screening themselves, given their

00:05:56.580 --> 00:05:59.800
importance to the health of everyone else
at the facility.

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:04.000
In fact, some countries’ Antarctic programs
even require their doctors to have their appendix

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:07.029
removed to prevent the possibility of appendicitis.

00:06:07.029 --> 00:06:12.310
Of course, a single doctor can’t know everything,
so for any advanced conditions that arise

00:06:12.310 --> 00:06:17.090
with station staff, the facility is outfitted
with advanced telemedicine equipment, which

00:06:17.090 --> 00:06:21.599
can transmit medical data in real-time, so
specialists in the US can attempt to diagnose

00:06:21.599 --> 00:06:24.080
and treat a patient remotely.

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:29.249
Of course, in order to do that they need internet,
which is yet another challenge at the South

00:06:29.249 --> 00:06:30.580
Pole.

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:35.379
Most non-polar, isolated research stations
nowadays are able to rely completely on commercial

00:06:35.379 --> 00:06:39.930
satellite internet providers, but that’s
not entirely possible at the bottom of the

00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:41.280
world.

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:44.810
Commercial satellite internet is only widespread
where there’s a market, and there’s only

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:47.509
a market where there are lots of people.

00:06:47.509 --> 00:06:51.729
That means that most telecommunications satellites
are not positioned in an orbit that the South

00:06:51.729 --> 00:06:53.939
Pole can reliably view.

00:06:53.939 --> 00:06:58.529
Therefore, the station has to largely count
on the NASA network of communications satellites

00:06:58.529 --> 00:07:00.770
designed to link spacecraft to earth.

00:07:00.770 --> 00:07:04.879
In fact, this network is the very one the
International Space Station uses for data

00:07:04.879 --> 00:07:06.499
up and downlink.

00:07:06.499 --> 00:07:10.840
Even this system, though, is only in view
of the pole a few times per day.

00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:14.729
On November 13th, 2020, for example, the South
Pole could connect to one of its satellites

00:07:14.729 --> 00:07:22.240
from 3:29 to 3:55 pm, 4:59 to 7:05 pm, and
8:09 to 9:27 pm, meaning they only had a connection

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:25.639
for three hours and fifty minutes that day.

00:07:25.639 --> 00:07:29.340
These NASA satellites provide the bulk of
the station’s data connection, with an overall

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:35.210
capacity of 275 Mbps, meaning they use these
short passes to transmit the bulk of the scientific

00:07:35.210 --> 00:07:39.150
data back to the outside world, and this is
also the time when it’s possible for station

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:43.069
staff to use the internet themselves to connect
with their families or friends.

00:07:43.069 --> 00:07:46.840
The station supplements this connection, though,
with a daily four and a half hour pass by

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:52.749
a UK Ministry of Defense communications satellite,
which provides a much leaner 1.54 Mbps data

00:07:52.749 --> 00:07:57.330
link, and a subsequent four-hour pass by a
US military communications satellite, which

00:07:57.330 --> 00:08:02.370
allows for 10 Mbps of uplink and 30 Mbps of
downlink.

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:07.389
For the rest of the day, the station only
has a very rudimentary 38 Kbps data link through

00:08:07.389 --> 00:08:11.889
the Iridium satellite network, meaning, for
all but the most urgent communications, it’s

00:08:11.889 --> 00:08:14.699
cut off from the world.

00:08:14.699 --> 00:08:18.999
Beyond that, even just taking out the trash
is difficult at the South Pole.

00:08:18.999 --> 00:08:23.259
The station is required by international law
to limit waste as much as possible, and this

00:08:23.259 --> 00:08:27.289
means that nearly everything brought in is
eventually brought out.

00:08:27.289 --> 00:08:31.249
All trash and recycling is collected during
the winter season, and flown back to the much

00:08:31.249 --> 00:08:33.200
larger McMurdo Station.

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:37.260
There, recycling is processed, while trash
is grouped together and stored to be sent

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:41.300
all the way back to the US on the single annual
resupply ship.

00:08:41.300 --> 00:08:46.190
That means that any garbage will travel 8,000
miles or 13,000 kilometers over a period of

00:08:46.190 --> 00:08:50.730
up to a year from a trash can at the South
Pole Station to a landfill in the US in order

00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:52.500
to be disposed of.

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:57.930
The reason for this obsessiveness around cleanliness
goes beyond environmental consciousness—it’s

00:08:57.930 --> 00:09:00.190
crucial to the science conducted there.

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:04.620
For example, there’s a zone that stretches
98 miles or 150 kilometers north-west of the

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:07.940
station where no motor vehicles at all are
allowed.

00:09:07.940 --> 00:09:12.120
That’s because this zone is upwind from
that station’s atmospheric research station,

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:16.510
which is located there due to the pollutant-free
air, and the exhaust of one snowmobile could

00:09:16.510 --> 00:09:21.000
contaminate the air enough to throw-off the
observations, potentially complicating some

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:22.000
research.

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:26.860
Now, it’s worth pointing out that this structure,
the South Pole station, is the only thing

00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:31.611
separating the staff of the facility from
the deadly Antarctic environment and, for

00:09:31.611 --> 00:09:36.300
nine months a year, the facility is entirely
unreachable.

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:40.080
Outside of the summer season, stretching from
November to mid-February, the weather is simply

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:45.031
too harsh for planes to land with any degree
of safety, so if this facility fails, there

00:09:45.031 --> 00:09:48.450
is almost zero opportunity for evacuation.

00:09:48.450 --> 00:09:53.070
To put this into perspective, if the International
Space Station fails, for example, astronauts

00:09:53.070 --> 00:09:57.310
can almost immediately load into the spacecraft
they came in and return to earth.

00:09:57.310 --> 00:10:01.830
At the South Pole, even if it were life or
death, and even if a pilot was willing to

00:10:01.830 --> 00:10:05.970
put their life on the line to get to the pole,
there are stretches of weeks when the weather

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:11.370
makes it physically impossible to get a plane
on the ground in one piece.

00:10:11.370 --> 00:10:16.200
However, in very limited circumstances, when
it becomes clear that someone has developed

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:20.140
a medical condition severe enough that they
will not make it till summer, and when there’s

00:10:20.140 --> 00:10:24.240
a flight crew willing to put their lives on
the line to save another’s, the Antarctic

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:27.520
Program has authorized attempts at evacuation.

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:31.000
This has only happened successfully three
times.

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:35.370
Each of these flights was conducted by extremely
experienced pilots from Ken Borek Air—a

00:10:35.370 --> 00:10:39.050
Canadian airline accustomed to operating at
the poles—in small Twin Otter aircraft—one

00:10:39.050 --> 00:10:42.390
of the only types that can withstand the Antarctic
winter cold.

00:10:42.390 --> 00:10:45.850
These aircraft had to be ferried down from
Canada, and then landed and held short of

00:10:45.850 --> 00:10:49.720
the continent for a number of days while waiting
for a weather window to fly further south

00:10:49.720 --> 00:10:51.000
to the pole.

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:55.160
Once that happened, though, they flew the
final stretch and landed in darkness, quickly

00:10:55.160 --> 00:10:59.260
picking up their patient before the aircraft
froze, and then flew to South America to reach

00:10:59.260 --> 00:11:01.020
an advanced hospital.

00:11:01.020 --> 00:11:05.160
With these evacuations, it took more than
a week to actually get an aircraft down to

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:09.910
the pole, so if something went catastrophically
wrong that made the entire station uninhabitable,

00:11:09.910 --> 00:11:14.980
such as a fire, for example, there’d be
little chance of getting everyone out in time.

00:11:14.980 --> 00:11:20.200
That’s why there’s a smaller, self-sufficient,
fireproof wing of the station designated as

00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:22.040
an emergency life-boat.

00:11:22.040 --> 00:11:26.640
This has a kitchen, a small living area, a
dedicated generator, a water treatment plant,

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:31.220
and is designed to be able to keep the station
staff alive through the winter, until an evacuation

00:11:31.220 --> 00:11:34.420
is possible, if the rest of the station fails.

00:11:34.420 --> 00:11:39.210
All in all, the 45 or so people living at
the South Pole over the winter have to be,

00:11:39.210 --> 00:11:44.030
in the most true sense possible, self-sufficient,
because there is almost no opportunity for

00:11:44.030 --> 00:11:48.920
outside help, and that’s exactly what makes
it so dangerous.

00:11:48.920 --> 00:11:53.130
This season, though, the potential for danger
is escalated.

00:11:53.130 --> 00:11:58.160
Right now, in November 2020, the South Pole
is reconnecting to the world for the first

00:11:58.160 --> 00:12:02.030
time since February 14th as its summer season
gets started.

00:12:02.030 --> 00:12:07.700
Now, the world has obviously changed a lot
between February and November, but Antarctica

00:12:07.700 --> 00:12:09.950
has pretty much stayed the same.

00:12:09.950 --> 00:12:14.280
Up until now, COVID was not a threat to the
continent, since it severed its link to the

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:17.380
world before the virus became widespread.

00:12:17.380 --> 00:12:22.010
Antarctica is currently the only COVID-free
continent, and it’s crucial it stays that

00:12:22.010 --> 00:12:23.010
way.

00:12:23.010 --> 00:12:27.420
In the cramped facilities of the various bases,
social distancing is impossible, one case

00:12:27.420 --> 00:12:31.720
can turn into a major outbreak very quickly,
and there’s really no ability for the intensive

00:12:31.720 --> 00:12:34.400
care that severe cases require.

00:12:34.400 --> 00:12:39.360
If the virus took hold in the Antarctic population,
a winter season, with limited possibility

00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:44.170
for evacuation, could become very difficult
or impossible.

00:12:44.170 --> 00:12:49.220
Therefore, all staff is now intensely tested
and quarantined for at least two weeks before

00:12:49.220 --> 00:12:53.070
flying to the continent, and the stations
are implementing policies such as mask-wearing

00:12:53.070 --> 00:12:54.810
for the first time as an extra-precaution.

00:12:54.810 --> 00:12:59.760
Also, this season’s activities have been
scaled back to essentially the bare minimum

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:03.920
required to keep the science going, such as
crucial maintenance and resupply, and so almost

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:06.860
all construction projects have been cancelled.

00:13:06.860 --> 00:13:12.390
That means that McMurdo station, for example,
will have a peak-population of just 450—compared

00:13:12.390 --> 00:13:14.110
to 1,250 normally.

00:13:14.110 --> 00:13:19.990
It’s tough to understate how tough it is
to live year-round at the South Pole, and

00:13:19.990 --> 00:13:23.400
COVID is making it even more challenging.

00:13:23.400 --> 00:13:27.430
Life at the bottom of the world is certainly
an adventure, but it’s one that requires

00:13:27.430 --> 00:13:32.670
spending the vast majority of the year indoors,
in a single building, with a small number

00:13:32.670 --> 00:13:33.670
of people.

00:13:33.670 --> 00:13:36.950
It is undoubtably a sacrifice.

00:13:36.950 --> 00:13:40.942
Most of those living and working there don’t
do so because it’s fun, because, for the

00:13:40.942 --> 00:13:47.190
most part, it’s not, but rather, they do
so because they believe so deeply and fundamentally

00:13:47.190 --> 00:13:54.350
that the science, even in the face of extreme
adversity, is worth it.

